(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cms-exam-5-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 5 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"When are palliative care discussions raised for elderly patients with cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Never in the elderly","At the first visit","After transplant only","In advanced stages"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Palliative care discussions belong to the advanced stages in the elderly, alongside tailored therapy, cautious diuretics and vasodilators, and a focus on quality of life. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"What is the most frequent presumed cause of myocarditis in developed countries?","choices":["Viral infection","Autoimmune disease","Bacterial infection","Drug reactions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In developed countries viral infection is presumed to be the most frequent cause, including parvovirus B19, enteroviruses such as Coxsackie group B, adenoviruses and hepatitis C. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man is recovering from a carotid endarterectomy for high-grade symptomatic stenosis. Which complications of the procedure should be monitored for?","choices":["Graft infection of the leg","Rupture into the retroperitoneum","Stroke or nerve damage","Wet gangrene of the foot"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Carotid endarterectomy removes plaque through a surgical incision and is the gold standard for high-grade stenosis; its named complications are stroke and nerve damage. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with viridans streptococcal endocarditis had positive blood cultures on days 1 and 2 and her first negative culture on day 4. She asks how long her antibiotics will last. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["10 days from the first dose","4-6 weeks counted from day 4","4-6 weeks from admission day","Until her fever settles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Therapy is typically 4-6 weeks counted from the first negative culture, here day 4, because all bacteria in the vegetation must be killed. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with restrictive cardiomyopathy has fatigue and exercise intolerance along with leg edema, hepatomegaly and ascites, but her lungs are relatively clear. Which pattern of heart failure is expected to dominate in this condition?","choices":["Isolated left-sided failure","High-output heart failure","Right-sided heart failure","Pure systolic failure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Although filling pressures are raised in both ventricles, right heart failure is often more prominent and may dominate in restrictive cardiomyopathy, giving edema, hepatomegaly and ascites. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with dilated cardiomyopathy and an ejection fraction of 30% is in sinus rhythm, has native heart valves and has no thrombus on echocardiogram. His neighbor with heart failure takes a blood thinner, and he asks whether he should too. Which response about anticoagulation is most accurate?","choices":["Needed by every dilated cardiomyopathy patient","Not needed in sinus rhythm without thrombus","Needed only after a heart transplant is done","Needed once ejection fraction falls below half"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In dilated cardiomyopathy anticoagulation is restricted to patients with atrial fibrillation, an artificial valve or a known mural thrombus. He has none of the three, so it is not indicated. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Which causes should be checked first in a patient with frequent premature ventricular contractions?","choices":["Hyperlipidemia, obesity","Dehydration, fever","Hypoxemia, electrolytes","Hypertension, diabetes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypoxemia and electrolyte imbalances (hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hypercalcemia) are the main causes, so new frequent premature ventricular contractions call for checking oxygen and electrolytes. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has a congenital arteriovenous fistula of the calf with numerous small communications, causing chronic swelling and aching. She has no signs of ischemia or heart failure. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Elastic support hose","Ligation of every communication","Surgical excision of the fistula","Lifelong anticoagulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Congenital fistulas are difficult to treat because the communications can be numerous, so they are managed conservatively with elastic support hose once peripheral artery disease is excluded. Embolization sometimes removes one. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Acute pericarditis is inflammation of the pericardial sac that lasts less than how long?","choices":["2 to 3 days","4 to 6 weeks","3 months","1 year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute pericarditis is inflammation of the pericardial sac lasting less than 4 to 6 weeks; symptoms beyond that without remission are called incessant, and beyond 3 months chronic. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with suspected tamponade undergoes right heart catheterization before drainage. Which pressure finding is expected?","choices":["Equal pressures in all chambers","A gradient across the aortic valve","An isolated high left atrial pressure","Low right atrial pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac catheterization in tamponade shows equalization of pressures in all chambers of the heart, because the pericardial fluid under pressure dictates diastolic pressure throughout. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which electrolyte is given as an intravenous push and then as an infusion to break torsades de pointes?","choices":["Phosphate","Calcium","Potassium","Magnesium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In a conscious patient, intravenous magnesium breaks torsades de pointes, and more magnesium is then given as an infusion; it is the main treatment. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which description fits a pericardial friction rub?","choices":["Basal crackles that clear after the patient coughs","Scratchy, triphasic, loudest at the left sternal border","Blowing holosystolic murmur radiating to the axilla","Low-pitched diastolic rumble heard best at the apex"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The rub is classically triphasic with a superficial scratchy or squeaking quality, waxes and wanes, is loudest over the left sternal border and is best heard with the diaphragm. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with long-standing hypertension has a routine electrocardiogram. The QRS is 0.16 seconds wide, with a dominant S wave in V1, a broad single-peaked R wave in leads I, V5 and V6, and no Q waves in those leads. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Right bundle branch block","First-degree block","Left bundle branch block","Ventricular tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A QRS over 0.12 seconds with a dominant S wave in V1, a monophasic R in the lateral leads and absent Q waves in I, V5 and V6 is left bundle branch block; chronic hypertension is a named cause. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which condition is a contraindication to anticoagulation for deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Extensive proximal clot","Active cancer","A recent long flight","Acute intracranial hemorrhage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anticoagulants are contraindicated with active bleeding, acute intracranial hemorrhage, major trauma or severe bleeding disorders, because they would worsen the bleeding. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man had a plaster cast removed from his right lower leg two days ago after a tibial fracture. He now has right calf swelling that measures 4 cm more than the left, and tenderness along the deep veins of the calf. Nothing else on the Wells criteria is present and no alternative diagnosis is as likely. What is his Wells score?","choices":["One point","Two points","Three points","Four points"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Recent plaster immobilization of the lower extremity, calf swelling over 3 cm and tenderness along the deep venous system are worth 1 point each, for 3. A score of 3 or more means high likelihood, so ultrasound is ordered. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has recurrent atrioventricular nodal reentry episodes that tend to start after coffee, alcohol or decongestant (sympathomimetic) use. She has no heart disease. Which counseling is most appropriate?","choices":["Avoid her known triggers","Start an anticoagulant","Expect a pacemaker","Avoid exercise"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nodal reentry may occur spontaneously or with exertion, caffeine, alcohol, beta-agonists and sympathomimetics, so limiting her known triggers is sensible counseling. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 37-year-old man with acute pericarditis feels better after 5 days of ibuprofen and colchicine and plans to stop both medicines now. Which counseling point is most important?","choices":["Finish the full course to prevent recurrence","Stopping now is safe since the pain is gone","Switch to prednisone to finish faster","Colchicine can stop; keep the ibuprofen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patient education stresses adherence to the full treatment course to prevent recurrence, which affects about 15 to 30% of patients, together with activity restriction and red-flag symptoms. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has Mobitz type II block found on a routine tracing; she has no symptoms. Her family prefers to avoid procedures. Which approach should be avoided?","choices":["Observation without pacing","Referral to cardiology","Permanent pacemaker placement","Reviewing her medication list"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Observation alone should be avoided because Mobitz type II is considered dangerous even without symptoms; its progression is unpredictable, so the definitive treatment is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 4-year-old with cyanotic congenital heart disease and a systemic-to-pulmonary shunt has 2 weeks of fever. The murmur sounds unchanged. Which finding would most suggest that the shunt is infected?","choices":["Rising blood pressure","An unchanged murmur","Falling oxygen saturation","Osler nodes on fingers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In children with cyanotic congenital heart disease and systemic-pulmonary shunts the murmur may not change; falling oxygen saturation can signal graft infection. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman whose hypotension was relieved by pericardiocentesis for a malignant effusion asks about her outlook. Which statement best describes her prognosis?","choices":["Her outlook matches viral cases","Volume drained sets her outlook","Drainage has cured her for good","The underlying cancer will drive it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. For cardiac tamponade the short-term outcome is often good with prompt treatment, but long-term prognosis depends on the underlying cause; a symptomatic malignant effusion has a median survival of 2 to 4 months. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman in torsades de pointes (a polymorphic rhythm whose QRS complexes twist around the baseline) becomes unresponsive with a blood pressure of 60/30 and only a faint pulse. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Transcutaneous pacing","Defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous lidocaine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A hemodynamically unstable patient in torsades de pointes is defibrillated (unsynchronized cardioversion); polymorphic rhythms are not synchronized. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman had a peripherally inserted central catheter placed in her right arm two weeks ago for long-term antibiotic therapy. She reports aching and swelling of the arm. Examination shows diffuse swelling of the entire right upper arm with mild redness, and there is no palpable cord and no purulent drainage. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassurance and arm elevation","Oral antibiotics for cellulitis","Venous duplex ultrasound of the arm","Warm compresses and ibuprofen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Upper extremity deep vein thrombosis often presents only with limb swelling and arm discomfort, and indwelling central venous catheters are a leading cause. Duplex ultrasound of the involved limb is the test. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with a medial ankle venous ulcer has used compression, elevation and wound care for five months and the ulcer has not healed. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Vascular specialist","Dermatologist","Physical therapist","Infectious disease specialist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nonhealing ulcers are referred to a vascular specialist, and an ulcer that has not healed in four to six months needs further workup for infection or other causes. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has had a resting heart rate of 105 to 115 for a year. Thyroid tests, a blood count, a drug screen and an echocardiogram are normal, and she takes no medications or stimulants. Her electrocardiogram shows a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atrial flutter with 2:1 block","Focal atrial tachycardia","Atrioventricular nodal reentry","Inappropriate sinus tachycardia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A sinus tachycardia that remains unexplained after secondary causes are excluded is inappropriate sinus tachycardia, a diagnosis of exclusion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman eight weeks postpartum has mild calf aching and a low pretest probability of deep vein thrombosis. Her clinician orders a D-dimer. Which characteristic makes a negative result useful for excluding a clot?","choices":["Measurement of vein compressibility","Direct visualization of clot","High sensitivity","High specificity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. D-dimer is sensitive but not specific, so a negative result in a low-likelihood patient effectively rules out deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism. It cannot confirm a clot, since fibrinolysis has many causes. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman developed chest pain and dyspnea during a hurricane evacuation. She has ST elevation, an elevated troponin and B-type natriuretic peptide, and an echocardiogram showing akinesis of the left ventricular apex. Which test confirms stress cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Exercise stress test","Endomyocardial biopsy","Cardiac catheterization","Serial troponin levels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis of Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy is confirmed with cardiac catheterization, which most often shows normal coronary arteries despite the infarct-like presentation. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 70-year-old smoker has a painful, dry, punched-out ulcer at the tip of his second toe with minimal bleeding and surrounding black necrotic tissue. It started as a minor scrape months ago. Which complication is he at risk of?","choices":["Osteomyelitis","Aortic dissection","Pseudoaneurysm formation","Venous insufficiency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ischemic skin ulcers fail to heal because blood supply cannot meet the demands of healing tissue; they sit at the most distal sites, are often ringed by dry gangrene, and can lead to osteomyelitis. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man with 3 months of weight loss presents with dyspnea and a large pericardial effusion. He has had no recent viral illness, chest surgery or radiation. Which etiology should be most strongly suspected?","choices":["Rheumatic fever","Malignancy","Dressler syndrome","Coxsackievirus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In older adults there is a higher index of suspicion for malignancy, especially with an effusion or tamponade, and his weight loss without a viral prodrome reinforces it. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"In tachy-brady syndrome, which tachyarrhythmia is most common?","choices":["Sinus tachycardia","Atrial flutter","Ventricular tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In tachy-brady syndrome a tachyarrhythmia, most commonly atrial fibrillation, alternates with symptomatic bradycardia or with offset pauses. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with confirmed constrictive pericarditis has failed a 3-month trial of anti-inflammatories and diuretics and remains fluid overloaded. Which service performs the definitive procedure she needs?","choices":["Cardiothoracic surgery","Gastroenterology","Interventional radiology","Nephrology"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Constriction refractory to medical therapy needs pericardiectomy, an operation performed by cardiothoracic surgery working with cardiology. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man has a regular narrow complex tachycardia at 190 with hidden P waves. He is alert with a blood pressure of 128/80. A properly performed vagal maneuver does not change the rhythm. Which is the next step?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Adenosine","Atropine","Intravenous magnesium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In stable supraventricular tachycardia, adenosine follows a failed vagal maneuver and may be repeated once; it may convert the rhythm or slow it enough to show P waves. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 20-year-old man has just been diagnosed with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy on echocardiography. He has two younger sisters and a brother. His mother had unexplained sudden death at 44. Which step for his family is most appropriate?","choices":["No family evaluation is needed","Evaluate his first-degree relatives","Test relatives only if symptomatic","Evaluate only female relatives"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is mostly inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern, so one affected parent is enough to pass it on, and detection in a family member is a common reason evaluation of relatives begins. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man is admitted with an inferior myocardial infarction. His heart rate falls to 46 with narrow QRS complexes and P waves that are either inverted (retrograde P waves) or buried in the QRS. Which rhythm is most likely?","choices":["Sinus bradycardia","Junctional rhythm","Atrial flutter","Focal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A regular narrow rhythm at 40 to 60 with inverted or buried P waves is a junctional rhythm, and inferior myocardial infarction is a listed cause. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with heart failure has palpitations. A 48-hour monitor shows several runs of 4 to 8 identical wide QRS beats, each lasting a few seconds, without hemodynamic compromise. Which is the first-line drug?","choices":["Digoxin","Adenosine","Intravenous magnesium","Beta blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Symptomatic non-sustained ventricular tachycardia is treated first with a beta blocker such as metoprolol or carvedilol. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man with frothy urine and leg swelling is found to have a moderate pericardial effusion. Which tests best evaluate the suspected cause?","choices":["Antinuclear antibody","Urine culture","Thyroid function tests","Albumin and urine protein"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Albumin and urine protein screen for nephrotic syndrome as a cause of pericardial effusion, alongside thyroid, rheumatologic, infectious and malignancy evaluation. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with extensive atherosclerosis develops painful, tender, bluish discoloration of several toes on his left foot, with localized pallor. His dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses are easily palpable. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Critical limb ischemia","Atheroembolism","Dry gangrene","Acute embolic limb ischemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Atheroemboli of fibrin, platelets and cholesterol debris from proximal plaques lodge in small vessels of muscle and skin, causing blue toe syndrome with tenderness and pallor while distal pulses usually stay palpable. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has had a peripheral intravenous catheter in his right forearm for six days. He now has a fever of 39.2 degrees Celsius, a tender cord along the vein, purulent drainage at the insertion site, and redness that extends several centimeters beyond the vein margin. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Uncomplicated superficial thrombophlebitis","Septic thrombophlebitis","Upper extremity deep vein thrombosis","Cellulitis alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. High fever, purulent drainage and erythema extending well beyond the margin of the vein after cannulation indicate infection within the vein, called suppurative or septic thrombophlebitis. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with a prosthetic valve and a past episode of anaphylaxis to penicillin needs a dental cleaning and can take pills. Which prophylactic agent should be avoided?","choices":["Doxycycline","Clarithromycin","Azithromycin","Cephalexin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cephalexin, cefazolin and ceftriaxone should not be used in patients with a history of anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria to penicillin or ampicillin. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man who smokes has a 4.3 cm asymptomatic thoracic aortic aneurysm and a blood pressure of 158/94. He asks what he can do to help himself. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Nothing changes until repair","Blood pressure matters only after surgery","Quit smoking; control blood pressure","Smoking matters only for leg disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Management of a thoracic aortic aneurysm includes smoking cessation, beta-adrenergic blockers and aggressive blood pressure control, with surveillance imaging for his size. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"How is an aneurysm defined?","choices":["Dilation over 10% of normal diameter","Any narrowing of the arterial lumen","Dilation over 50% of normal diameter","A tear through the intima alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An aneurysm is a pathologic dilation of a segment of a blood vessel, defined as localized dilation of an arterial segment greater than 50% of its normal diameter. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A perivalvular abscess that extends into the epicardium causes which complication?","choices":["Pericarditis","Atrial myxoma","Heart block","Myocardial infarction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Extension of a perivalvular abscess into the epicardium causes pericarditis; extension into the upper ventricular septum interrupts conduction. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has sudden chest pain and diaphoresis an hour after learning she has lost her savings in a financial collapse. The electrocardiogram shows ST elevation and troponin is elevated. No catheterization has been done yet. Which is the most appropriate initial approach?","choices":["Follow acute coronary syndrome protocols","Discharge home with grief counseling","Start a positive inotrope infusion","Alcohol ablation of the septum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stress cardiomyopathy mimics acute coronary syndrome, and because there are no reliable early differences, evaluation and treatment follow the established protocols for acute coronary syndrome patients. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a slow sinus rhythm at 38 six hours after an inferior myocardial infarction. She feels faint and her blood pressure is 80/50. Each narrow QRS has a normal P wave before it. Which drug should be given first?","choices":["Diltiazem","Atropine","Metoprolol","Adenosine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable sinus bradycardia, here from an infarction affecting the conduction tissue, is treated first with atropine, then a temporary pacemaker. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has 6 weeks of aching and stiffness in her shoulders and hips, fatigue and a low-grade fever. Her erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein are high, but no murmur is heard and three sets of blood cultures are negative. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Antiphospholipid syndrome","Subacute endocarditis","Atrial myxoma","Polymyalgia rheumatica"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Polymyalgia rheumatica sits in the differential of endocarditis because it causes myalgia with elevated inflammatory markers, but there is no murmur and cultures are negative. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with long-standing diabetes describes a continuous burning sensation in both feet that is the same at rest and during exercise. Her pedal pulses are easily palpable. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Peripheral neuropathy","Peripheral artery disease","Critical limb ischemia","Spinal stenosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Peripheral neuropathy, common in older patients with diabetes or alcoholism, causes a continuous burning sensation in the foot that is unaffected by exercise, unlike arterial claudication. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation at 150 has crackles throughout both lungs from pulmonary edema and a blood pressure of 82/50. Which feature makes this presentation a medical emergency?","choices":["Her age over 65","An irregularly irregular pulse","Severe heart failure","A heart rate above 100"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Medical emergencies from atrial fibrillation include severe heart failure (pulmonary edema, hypotension) and atrial fibrillation with an acute myocardial infarction. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with hypertension has an acute aortic dissection with severe tearing chest pain and a blood pressure of 176/100. Which intravenous medication should be started first to lower her pressure?","choices":["Cilostazol","Esmolol","Alteplase","Heparin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A beta blocker such as esmolol is given first, lowering heart rate and shear force to bring systolic pressure to 100 to 120 mmHg; a vasodilator such as nitroprusside is added only if pressure stays high. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has just undergone alcohol septal ablation, in which alcohol is injected through a catheter into a septal branch of the coronary artery. He asks when he can expect his symptoms to improve. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["Never; it is a diagnostic test","Only after a repeat injection","Over months as the septum thins","Within minutes of the injection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Alcohol septal ablation induces a therapeutic infarction of the proximal septum, and it takes months for the septum to thin and the outflow obstruction to ease. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has 3 days of fever to 40.0 °C (104.0 °F), rigors and a new murmur, and he looks toxic. Which organism is a classic cause of this tempo of infective endocarditis?","choices":["Coagulase-negative staphylococci","Viridans streptococci","Staphylococcus aureus","Gram-negative coccobacilli"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An abrupt, toxic, high-fever course is acute endocarditis, caused by virulent organisms such as Staphylococcus aureus and beta-hemolytic streptococci. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man has an incidental finding on a preoperative electrocardiogram: a constant PR interval in conducted beats with occasional P waves that are not followed by a QRS complex, and no PR lengthening before the dropped beats. He has no symptoms. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repeat the tracing in a year","Permanent pacemaker","Reassurance and follow-up","Start an oral beta blocker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type II is considered dangerous even without symptoms, because of unpredictable progression to complete block and a poor prognosis, so the definitive treatment is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man in ventricular fibrillation arrest is shocked several times, and his rhythm changes to a flat line with no electrical activity in two leads. What does this change mean for his outlook?","choices":["Successful defibrillation","A better chance of survival","No change in prognosis","A much worse prognosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ventricular tachycardia and fibrillation have the best prognosis of the arrest rhythms; asystole has a poor prognosis and is usually an end-of-life rhythm. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman who delivered a healthy baby three months ago now has breathlessness on exertion, orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat) and swollen ankles. She had no heart disease before pregnancy. Echocardiogram shows a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 30%. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Stress cardiomyopathy","Peripartum cardiomyopathy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Peripartum cardiomyopathy is a rare form of dilated cardiomyopathy that develops in the last trimester of pregnancy or within the first six months after delivery, as it has here. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man is found on the floor two days after collapsing. His right leg has profound sensory loss, paralysis with rigid muscles, and no arterial or venous Doppler signal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Surgical thromboembolectomy","Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Urgent computed tomography angiography","Major amputation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An irreversibly ischemic limb has major tissue loss or permanent nerve damage, profound sensory loss, paralysis and inaudible arterial and venous signals; nonviable limbs require major amputation whatever therapy is used. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has Wenckebach block (a PR interval that grows longer until a beat is dropped) found on a routine tracing. She feels well and asks what would make her a candidate for a pacemaker. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["Being over 65 years old","Dropped beats during sleep","Symptoms such as fainting","A PR above 0.20 seconds"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type I needs no treatment unless symptoms are debilitating; a permanent pacemaker is considered for symptomatic patients, such as those with dizziness, exertional intolerance or syncope. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which laboratory finding counts as an immunologic phenomenon among the minor modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Rheumatoid factor","Normocytic anemia","Pyuria","Leukocytosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The immunologic phenomena are glomerulonephritis, Osler nodes, Roth spots and rheumatoid factor; rheumatoid factor is the laboratory member of that minor criterion. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman who is 34 weeks pregnant sits in a car for nine hours and develops left calf swelling and pain. Her pregnancy supplies a hypercoagulable state and the long ride supplies venous stasis. Which additional component would complete the Virchow triad?","choices":["Endothelial trauma","Hypercoagulable state","Venous stasis","Arterial thrombosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Virchow triad is venous stasis, hypercoagulable state and endothelial trauma. Pregnancy and the car ride supply two components, so the missing one is injury to the vein wall, as from trauma, surgery, burns or injection. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"How is hemodynamically stable atrial flutter treated first?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Beta or calcium channel blocker","Repeated adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stable atrial flutter is rate-controlled with a beta blocker or calcium channel blocker; an unstable patient gets synchronized cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man whose Wolff-Parkinson-White was found on a school electrocardiogram presents with palpitations. The rhythm is irregular and wide-complex, at rates up to 260, with varying delta waves (slurred upstrokes). He is alert with a blood pressure of 118/74. Which drug is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Atropine","Adenosine","Intravenous procainamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Irregular, wide, very fast complexes in a patient with Wolff-Parkinson-White are pre-excited atrial fibrillation, treated with intravenous procainamide; adenosine must never be used. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has had 6 weeks of low-grade fever and weight loss and has a new murmur. Subacute endocarditis is suspected. Which complete blood count finding is most likely?","choices":["Normocytic anemia","Microcytic anemia","Eosinophilia","Polycythemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The complete blood count often shows leukocytosis and anemia of chronic disease with a normal mean corpuscular volume (normocytic). — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man was treated for acute pericarditis 3 months ago and has been symptom-free for 6 weeks. His sharp, positional chest pain has now returned with a friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound). C-reactive protein is mildly raised. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Prednisone alone for 6 months","Ibuprofen with colchicine, stopped at 2 weeks","Anakinra plus prednisone at once","Ibuprofen with colchicine for 6 months or more"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is a first recurrence, symptoms returning after a symptom-free interval of 4 to 6 weeks or longer, treated with a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug plus colchicine for 6 months or more. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"}]};
